{"content":"q28.\n\n**Runners the server actually starts** (all wiring in `server/server.go:153-174` `startBackgroundRunners`, called from `Server.Start` at `server/server.go:129`):\n\n- **`s3presign` runner (`server/runner/s3presign/runner.go`) — RunOnce at startup, then a 12-hour `time.Ticker`.** `s3presignRunner.RunOnce(ctx)` fires synchronously during `Start` (`server/server.go:163`), then `Run` loops on `time.NewTicker(runnerInterval)` with `runnerInterval = 12h` (`server/runner/s3presign/runner.go:27-41`), exiting on its own cancellable child context (`s3Context`, `server/server.go:156`) and tracked by `backgroundRunnerWG` for graceful shutdown.\n\nThat is the **only** entry in `startBackgroundRunners` — the graph confirms it: callees of `Server.Start` reach `s3presign.NewRunner`/`Run`/`RunOnce` and nothing else runner-shaped.\n\n**Also-running background goroutines, but not scheduled/registered by the server:**\n\n- **email async worker pool (2 goroutines) — package `init()`, queue-driven.** `internal/email/email.go:14-36`: two goroutines drain `asyncEmailQueue` (buffer 128). Started at import time, not by `Server.Start`; triggered by enqueue, not by a clock.\n- **webhook async dispatch pool (4 goroutines) — package `init()`, queue-driven.** `internal/webhook/webhook.go:39-55`: four goroutines drain `asyncPostQueue` (buffer 128). Same story — import-time, event-driven.\n- **HTTP serve goroutine — one-shot.** `server/server.go:124-128`, `httpServer.Serve(listener)`.\n- **SSE heartbeat ticker — per-request, not a background runner.** `server/router/api/v1/sse_handler.go:63-65`, lives for the duration of one SSE connection.\n- **PAT last-used write — fire-and-forget per authentication.** `server/auth/authenticator.go:179-185`.\n\n**Unused scheduling infrastructure:**\n\n- **`internal/scheduler` — a full cron scheduler that the server never uses.** The package provides cron-expression parsing (`internal/scheduler/parser.go:30` `ParseCronExpression`, 5- and 6-field), job registration/validation (`internal/scheduler/job.go`), a run loop (`internal/scheduler/scheduler.go`), and middleware (`Recovery`, `Logging`, `Timeout` — `internal/scheduler/middleware.go`), documented as \"GitHub Actions-inspired cron job scheduler\" (`internal/scheduler/doc.go:1`). Every reference to it is inside the package's own tests and docs (`example_test.go`, `integration_test.go`, `doc.go`) — no production call site. The graph returns a certified zero for callers of `scheduler.New` and `Scheduler.Start`. So the `s3presign` runner hand-rolls its own `time.Ticker` instead of using the cron infrastructure sitting next to it.\n- **`memopayload` runner (`server/runner/memopayload/runner.go:27` `RunOnce`) is never started either** — no scheduling at all. The graph shows zero callers of `memopayload.NewRunner`/`RunOnce`; only the free function `RebuildMemoPayload` is used, called inline on the request path in `server/router/api/v1/memo_service.go:108` (create) and `:447` (update). The batch-rebuild-all-memos runner is effectively dead code.","iteration":12,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
